.TH DMOST "6" "November 2026" "Dueling Masters of Space Time" "Games"
.SH NAME
dmost \- Dueling Masters of Space Time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dmost
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Dueling Masters of Space Time is a two-player turn-based strategy board game
that could be described as a cross between chess, Battleship, and Stratego.
This SDL2 application allows you to play against a built-in computer opponent
(the default) or offline against a human opponent.
.PP
Each player has a representation of the board, which consists of a 10x10 grid
of squares.
Each square has a name formed by combining the row
.RB ( A \- J )
and column
.RB ( 1 \- 10 ).
Initially, Red is assigned rows
.BR A \- E
(and moves first), and Blue is assigned rows
.BR F \- J .
Each player secretly deploys 18 pieces on their half of the board:
1 W-fighter
.RB ( W ),
1 B-fighter
.RB ( B ),
2 X-fighters
.RB ( X1 ", " X2 ),
2 Y-fighters
.RB ( Y1 ", " Y2 ),
4 Z-fighters
.RB ( Z1 \- Z4 ),
7 immobile asteroids
.RB ( A ),
and 1 immobile Home Planet
.RB ( HP ).
.PP
During setup, asteroids and the Home Planet must occupy squares by themselves,
while friendly fighters may stack on the same square.
Pieces may traverse friendly fighters, but cannot pass through their own
asteroids or Home Planet.
The W-fighter moves in a 2-then-1 L-shaped path (like a chess knight,
traversing two squares along the longer axis and then one perpendicular
square, with intermediate squares checked for obstacles and collisions).
The B-fighter and Z-fighters move like chess queens, X-fighters move like
chess rooks, and Y-fighters move like chess bishops.
Asteroids and the Home Planet are immobile.
.PP
Among mobile fighters and the Home Planet, combat strength from strongest to
weakest is:
.BR W ", " B ", " X ", " Y ", " Z ", " HP .
In a collision, the stronger piece destroys the weaker piece, and equal-strength
fighters destroy each other.
Asteroids act as mines: they destroy any piece that collides with them and are
destroyed themselves.
.PP
When a player moves a piece, only the identity of the piece and its departure
square are announced to the opponent, never its destination.
The piece is then considered "in transit" until an attempt is made to move it
again.
When a piece is moved again, its previous trajectory is checked against the
opponent's board, and any collisions are resolved recursively.
.PP
It is thus possible to intercept a piece after it has already been moved (while
it is in transit), or likewise to dodge out of the way before a piece's path
is resolved.
.PP
The object of the game is to destroy your opponent's Home Planet by ramming
into it with one of your pieces.
A game ends in a draw if neither player can destroy the other's Home Planet,
such as when both players pass consecutively with no legal moves, when neither
player has any mobile pieces remaining, or when only Y-fighters remain and are
trapped on square colors (parities) that cannot reach the opposing Home Planet.
.SH "GAME MODES"
.TP
.B vs Computer (Default)
Play against a built-in, non-cheating AI opponent backed by the same
private-board rules engine and message protocol as the tabletop game.
The AI sees only announced departure squares and collision results, never
your hidden board.
Setup rules, legal moves, turn order, recursive collision resolution, pass
turns, and victory/draw detection are enforced automatically.
.IP
While holding a piece, legal destination squares are highlighted in green.
In-transit pieces are drawn with a dashed ring, movement trails show prior
positions, and enemy-territory squares confirmed clear are shaded green.
Opponent moves and collision replays are animated (including W-fighter
L-paths), with reveal pulses for newly spotted enemy pieces and amber
starbursts at collision sites.
Destroyed pieces remain visible at their collision squares as semi-transparent
crossed-out markers, recent collision squares are outlined in amber, and a bold
collision banner appears above the scrollable combat/event log.
When the game ends, a bottom-margin banner and a Game Over dialog allow you
to
.B Play Again
or
.B View Board
to inspect the final position.
.TP
.B Manual (Offline)
Traditional tabletop helper mode for playing against a human opponent over
voice, chat, or across a room using separate boards.
Players announce departures externally and move and track pieces manually;
rules and collisions are not enforced automatically.
.TP
.B Free form
Unconstrained board mode with no setup or movement rules enforced.
Selecting
.B Free form
pauses an active
.B vs Computer
match without resetting it.
.SH CONTROLS
.SS "Toolbar and Status Bar"
.TP
.B Mode selector
Choose between
.B Board Setup
(arrange your pieces; returning here after starting a match resets it),
.B Play Game
(lock in setup and start or resume play),
.B Free form
(unconstrained movement), and
.B Begin New Game
(reset the board).
.TP
.B Opponent selector
Switch between
.B vs Computer
and
.B Manual (Offline)
(resets the board).
.TP
.B Side selector
Choose
.B Play as Red
(moves first) or
.B Play as Blue
(opponent moves first).
.TP
.B Auto Setup
Randomly arranges all 18 of your pieces in a legal defensive/scouting
formation (available during setup in
.B vs Computer
mode).
.TP
.B Begin New Game
Resets the board and starts a new match (also appears in the status bar at
Game Over).
.TP
.B Invert Board
Flips the board vertically so your half can be viewed at the bottom or top.
.TP
.B Help
Opens the in-game instructions dialog.
.TP
.B Quit Dueling
Exits
.BR dmost .
.TP
.B Pass Turn
Located in the status bar; enabled during your turn in
.B vs Computer
mode only when you have no legal moves available.
.SS "Mouse and Keyboard"
.TP
.B Left-click
Pick up a piece, then left-click a destination square to place or move it (or
click the right-hand piece tray during setup or manual/free mode to take it
off the board).
In
.B vs Computer
mode during your turn, clicking a piece also interrupts any remaining queued
animations.
.TP
.B Right-click
Release a currently held piece without moving it, or toggle a personal marker
on any board square.
.TP
.B Piece tray
During play, moving the pointer near the right edge of the board slides open
the off-board piece tray; moving back to the left collapses it.
.TP
.B F11
Toggle fullscreen mode.
.TP
.B Escape
Close an open dialog or menu, or release a currently held piece without moving it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I pinwheel.png
Optional galaxy background image, loaded from
.I pinwheel.png
or
.I src/pinwheel.png
relative to the current working directory if present.
